+\subsection{Cross-fitted scores and covariate-specific genotype distributions: prespecified validation}
+\label{sec:followup-results}
+
+The follow-ups of Section~\ref{sec:crossfit} were validated on the samples
+of Section~\ref{sec:weights-binary-results}, regenerated from their seeds
+(archive \code{20261006-}\allowbreak\code{hratt-followups}, protocol frozen
+before the main run). The new cells were mixed traits at $F_{ST}=0.05$ and
+variants of the structured cells with 10 principal components among the
+covariates. All 660 cases completed, and five of seven criteria passed.
+
+\paragraph{Effects.}
+Cross-fitting removed the attenuation (Table~\ref{tab:followup-effects}):
+\begin{itemize}
+ \item Without structure or selection on the outcome (S0--S2), HRATT's
+  effects were within 1\% of the population slopes. Unweighted, it was 8\%
+  low under selection on the outcome, as the design expects.
+ \item At $F_{ST}=0.05$ without principal components they were 2.0\% below the
+  within-population slopes.
+ \item In-sample scores gave $-28$ to $-36$\%.
+ \item Under selection on the outcome (S3), weighted HRATT was 5.4\% low
+  (Monte Carlo SE 2.1), failing that criterion. Weighted least squares was
+  4.0\% low on the same samples, so inverse-probability weighting, not
+  HRATT, accounts for most of the gap.
+ \item Binary one-step effects were within 3\% of the population marginal log
+  odds ratios, with Monte Carlo SEs of 4--9\%; in-sample scores gave
+  $-16$\% and $-18$\%.
+\end{itemize}
+
+Power did not change: mean QTL $\chi^2$ was 0.999--1.009 times that of
+in-sample scores. Weighted HRATT exceeded weighted least squares by 1.20 to
+1.29 times, and binary HRATT exceeded logistic regression by 1.04 times.
+
+\paragraph{Calibration.}
+Without ancestry-dependent weights every test stayed calibrated
+(Table~\ref{tab:followup-calibration}):
+\begin{itemize}
+ \item Quantitative $\lgc$ was 0.993--1.008, with 0.92--1.04 times the nominal
+  rate at $10^{-3}$.
+ \item Binary two-sided rates were 0.94--1.08 at $10^{-3}$ and 0.84--1.14 at
+  $10^{-4}$. At 1\% prevalence no replicate failed (2 of 30 on 5 October).
+ \item The doubled tails put about half of $\alpha/2$ in the upper tail at 1\%
+  prevalence (0.51 at $10^{-3}$), at the edge of the criterion's range.
+ \item On chromosome 10 of the mixed traits $\lgc$ was 0.971--1.024. This
+  includes the refitted scores with $\lambda$ at $F_{ST}=0.05$.
+\end{itemize}
+
+With weights that depend on ancestry (S4) and principal components among
+the covariates, weighted tests rejected low-frequency variants at 0.85
+(quantitative) and 0.96 (binary) times the nominal rate at $10^{-3}$. The
+pooled-frequency model gave 6.54 and 3.96 with the same covariates, and
+the new model without principal components 5.86 and 3.16.
+
+\paragraph{Time.}
+Cross-fitting took 1.11 (quantitative) and 1.08 (binary) times as long as
+in-sample scores on the same cases. Weighted HRATT took 1.55 times as long as
+HRATT-HE, excluding the saddlepoint, failing the limit of 1.3; it was 1.32
+on 5 October, before the covariate-specific frequencies.
+
